*  GIT.FURNIDS(REPO, CANDS, IDS) — which of CANDS are account furniture.
*  Copyright (C) 2026 Gordon Heydon.  GPL-2.0-only (see LICENSE).
*
*  ONE PLACE THAT ASKS THE QUESTION, for the same reason GIT.FILECLASS exists:
*  `add` and `status` have to agree exactly, or the account never reads clean.
*  When add dropped VOC's pointers to furniture (mv_git#78) and status did not
*  know to, every one came back as `?? VOC/_HOLD_` — untracked forever, because
*  the thing reporting them was the thing that would never stage them.
*
*  THE CALLER SUPPLIES THE CANDIDATES.  This used to walk VOC itself, which
*  meant walking it twice on every add and put a select in a program that did
*  not need one — and the MVX compiler rejected the loop, which is how the
*  duplication announced itself.  `add` already has the list; `status` builds
*  one with the select spelling it already uses.  CANDS is @AM-separated, each
*  entry either a bare id or `id<VM>type`.
*
*  The rules themselves are NOT here.  They live in mv_account_furniture() in
*  the engine, one table per MV system, reached through the FURNITURE op — which
*  matches strings and reads no records, so mvgitd can answer it on UniVerse
*  just as in-session CallC does on UniData (mv_git#81).
      SUBROUTINE GIT.FURNIDS(REPO, CANDS, IDS)
      $INCLUDE BP.INC PLATFORM.H
      AM = CHAR(254) ; VM = CHAR(253)
      IDS = ""
      IF CANDS = "" THEN RETURN
      FOUT = ""
      CALL GIT.OBJ("FURNITURE", 2, REPO, CANDS, "", "", "", FOUT)
*     The engine answers "" only on failure, and any account keeps at least VOC,
*     so an empty answer means the op did not run — report no furniture rather
*     than declare every candidate furniture.
      IF FOUT = "" THEN RETURN
      NC = DCOUNT(CANDS, AM)
      FOR FK = 1 TO NC
         LOCATE(CANDS<FK>, FOUT; FPOS) ELSE
            IDS<-1> = FIELD(CANDS<FK>, VM, 1)
         END
      NEXT FK
      RETURN
      END
